@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+# PostgreSQL retry switch
+
+- Configure durable Postgres storage with `DATABASE_URL` (or standard `PG*` variables) and `SOURCES`. Redis is no longer required. Stop the old retry workers before starting the Postgres workers; the production retry backfill is already complete, so do not import those rows again.
+- Apply missing migrations in order: `001_deliveries.sql`, `002_attempt_history.sql`, then `003_pending_lookup.sql`. Existing installations with the first two applied need only `psql "$DATABASE_URL" -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f migrations/003_pending_lookup.sql`. Run migration 003 outside a transaction; concurrent index creation keeps writes available and `ANALYZE` refreshes estimates after the backfill. It ensures the pending index exists; installations already using it retain the same claim query and index.
+- Build with `npm ci && npm run build`, then start with `npm start`. Verify a signed webhook returns `202`/`pending` and becomes delivered; monitor worker errors and overdue pending rows. Restrict `/admin/*` through deployment access controls.
+- Retries survive restarts: only 5xx responses retry, with five total attempts and default delays of 100/200/400/800 ms. Optional per-source `retryDelaysMs` supplies four integer millisecond delays. Existing scheduled times remain unchanged. Claims recover after 60 seconds; requests time out after 30 seconds. Recipients must deduplicate `x-relay-delivery` because interrupted requests can repeat.
+- On rollback, stop the new workers and retain the database and pending rows. Resume with a compatible Postgres worker; an older Redis worker cannot consume this queue. Do not reset attempts or replay the completed backfill.
